WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.759
Bayesian networks can be built from expertise, data, or both.

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:06.320
Expert-driven modeling starts with the reasoning purpose.

00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:10.344
What decision, diagnosis, forecast, or risk question should the model support?

00:00:10.344 --> 00:00:14.368
Then the team selects variables at the right level of detail.

00:00:14.368 --> 00:00:18.027
A model that is too coarse may hide important differences.

00:00:18.027 --> 00:00:23.149
A model that is too detailed may become hard to estimate, explain, and maintain.

00:00:23.149 --> 00:00:24.612
After variables are selected,

00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:29.002
modelers draw plausible dependency relationships and confirm that the graph is acyclic.

00:00:29.002 --> 00:00:32.661
They estimate local probability distributions, review assumptions with domain experts,

00:00:32.661 --> 00:00:35.222
and test the model with representative scenarios.

00:00:35.222 --> 00:00:39.612
In an equipment model, engineers may know which components influence which alarms.

00:00:39.612 --> 00:00:41.076
In a security model,

00:00:41.076 --> 00:00:45.100
analysts may know which telemetry is informative for which incident hypotheses.

00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:49.856
Expert knowledge can be valuable, but it should still be documented and tested.

00:00:49.856 --> 00:00:52.051
Data-driven learning has two broad forms.

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:56.076
Parameter learning estimates probability values when the graph structure is known.

00:00:56.076 --> 00:00:58.271
If the network structure is accepted,

00:00:58.271 --> 00:01:03.027
historical data can help estimate the CPT rows or other local distribution parameters.

00:01:03.027 --> 00:01:06.320
Structure learning searches for plausible graph structures from data.

00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:11.441
It can suggest dependencies that human experts missed, but it can also reflect bias,

00:01:11.441 --> 00:01:14.002
missing data, hidden variables, or accidental correlations.

00:01:14.002 --> 00:01:16.563
Hybrid approaches combine expert knowledge and data.

00:01:16.563 --> 00:01:20.954
Experts may constrain which edges are allowed, and data may estimate parameters.

00:01:20.954 --> 00:01:23.515
Learned structure does not automatically prove causality.

00:01:23.515 --> 00:01:26.807
Different network structures can sometimes represent similar statistical relationships.

00:01:26.807 --> 00:01:30.100
A graph edge means the model includes a dependency.

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:32.661
Causal interpretation requires stronger assumptions, careful design,

00:01:32.661 --> 00:01:34.490
and evidence beyond the graph.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:37.417
Domain review, scenario testing, and validation remain necessary.

00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:39.612
The workflow should also include documentation.

00:01:39.612 --> 00:01:44.734
Record the purpose of the network, the meaning of each variable, the allowed states,

00:01:44.734 --> 00:01:48.759
the source of each parameter, and the reason for important edges.

00:01:48.759 --> 00:01:52.051
Version the model when the graph or parameters change.

00:01:52.051 --> 00:01:56.807
If the network supports recurring decisions, keep example cases that show expected behavior.

00:01:56.807 --> 00:02:00.100
Those examples become regression tests for the model's reasoning.
